# The two shapes the handle form REFUSES, and each is refused rather than
# implemented on the author's decision of 2026-09-13 narrowing panel 145.
#
# A `record` inside an `extern` group with a `tag` and no fields is a handle: a
# pointer to a type the header names and leaves opaque. What it may not do:
#
#   - be a map KEY, directly or through a record that holds one. A handle is an
#     address C owns, and C recycles an address — close one and the next
#     allocation can hand back the same bytes, so a value stored under the
#     closed handle is found again by a handle that has nothing to do with it.
#     The sitting that adopted the form did not rule on hashing at all, so the
#     language refuses the shape rather than choosing an answer nobody argued.
#   - share its TAG with another HANDLE. Two handles over one tag are two Heroes
#     types and ONE C type — `sqlite3 *` twice — so this compiler refuses handing
#     one where the other belongs and clang accepts it: the mutant survives every
#     instrument the language has, permanently.
#
# **And one shape that was refused until 2026-09-15 and is not a refusal at
# all**: a handle beside a record WITH FIELDS on one tag. Those are `struct s *`
# and `struct s`, two C types, and clang refuses a swap between them unaided —
# measured, two errors in either direction. Panel 153 narrowed the rule to one
# of each KIND, because the pair it was refusing by accident is how a struct C
# hands back by pointer is both pointed at and read. The legal pair is
# `tests/golden/run/fixedbugs-a-tag-names-a-handle-and-a-record.hero`.
extern "sqlite3.h" link "sqlite3"
    record Db tag sqlite3
    record Stmt tag sqlite3_stmt
    record Db2 tag sqlite3  #~ duplicate_tag
    function sqlite3_close(db: Db) -> i64
    function sqlite3_finalize(statement: Stmt) -> i64

# Two records WITH FIELDS over one tag are the same refusal seen from the other
# side: both are `struct tm`, and nothing tells them apart.
extern "time.h"
    record Broken tag tm partial
        tm_year: i32
    record Broken2 tag tm partial  #~ duplicate_tag
        tm_year: i32

# A record that HOLDS a handle carries the refusal with it, and the message
# names the field that reaches one.
record Conn
    handle: Db

function main()
    direct: {Db: i64} @ {}  #~ handle_map_key
    held: {Conn: i64} @ {}  #~ handle_map_key
    print((direct.len() + held.len()).to_str())
